{
  "dom_id": "step-q49086",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/5a753c70df3b523ccdeead554bee653ebba355d41ef95aea7ace17236fd38829.png)\n\nThe graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=g(x)$, is shown above. Of the following, which best describes the function $y=g(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $y={g}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis (where we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0$) twice, at the points $A$ and $B$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/42e073c044b6bf9ced663bcb55bbd89d7ac79f85f918ca3364d2be3b2f8e74bc.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=g(x)$ occurs when the graph $y={g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Hence, we have an extremum at $B$ only. This is a relative maximum since the derivative goes from positive to negative.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is horizontal) or $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). These are points the $A$, $C$ and $D$ on the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200385/assets/650e68ff0dfbb6f0b679559a46532f2e29fb970f392a6bd4932b8363c2aa6777.png)\n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y={g}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have an inflection point at $A$, where the slope goes from negative to positive, and an inflection point at $C$, where the slope goes from positive to negative.\n\nTherefore, we get one relative maximum and two inflection points on $[-4,4]$ for $y=g(x).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
